##! Please refer to the documentation at
##! https://coreruleset.org/docs/development/regex_assembly/.

##! Rule 944160: Malformed Base64 padding (Java4Shell)
##!
##! Java4Shell is an out-of-bounds heap write in the HotSpot x86-64
##! AVX-512 VBMI Base64 decoder intrinsic. A miscomputed output mask in the
##! tail handling is triggered by padding characters. The published trigger
##! is 64 `A` characters followed by a single `=` (128 `A` + `=` on OpenJDK 17).
##!
##! This expression is the first step of a chained rule. It selects values
##! that consist of exactly one Base64 or Base64url token of at least 64
##! characters followed by `=` padding. The chained rule then checks the
##! alignment with `t:length`: valid padded Base64 always has a length that
##! is a multiple of 4.
##!
##! Design notes:
##! - Only whole values are selected. Inside a longer value the start of the
##!   token is ambiguous, because `-`, `_`, `+` and `/` are both alphabet
##!   characters and common separators (SRI hashes, URL paths), so the
##!   alignment cannot be determined reliably.
##! - The two alphabets are separate alternatives: no JDK decoder accepts a
##!   mix of `+/` and `-_`.
##! - Only single-character repeats are used, no repeated groups. PCRE can
##!   match these without backtracking, so long values do not exceed the
##!   PCRE match limit. The expression is also RE2 compatible.
##!
##! Ref: https://calif.io/java4shell

##!^ ^
##!$ =+$

##! Standard alphabet (`Base64.getDecoder()`)
(?:[A-Za-z0-9+/]{64,})
##! URL-safe alphabet (`Base64.getUrlDecoder()`)
(?:[A-Za-z0-9_\-]{64,})
